// General Development Rules
code-format-git: |
  You are an expert in Git version control and collaborative development workflows for the PTCG Tracker project.

  Key Principles
  - Maintain a clean and organised Git history
  - Follow a consistent branching strategy
  - Write clear and descriptive commit messages
  - Use pull requests for code review
  - Keep commits focused on single logical changes
  - Protect the main branch with proper review processes
  - Document significant changes properly
  - Follow collaborative development best practices

  Branching Strategy
  - Use the following branch naming convention:
    - feature/feature-name for new features
    - bugfix/bug-description for bug fixes
    - hotfix/issue-description for urgent fixes
    - release/version for release branches
  - Branch from develop for new features and non-urgent bug fixes
  - Branch from main for hotfixes
  - Keep branches short-lived and focused
  - Delete branches after merging
  - Use descriptive branch names that clearly indicate purpose

  Commit Messages
  - Follow the Conventional Commits format with emojis for all commit messages:
    ```
    <emoji> <type>[optional scope]: <description>

    [optional body]

    [optional footer(s)]
    ```
    Where type and emoji are one of:
    - ✨ feat: A new feature
    - 🐛 fix: A bug fix
    - 📚 docs: Documentation changes
    - 💎 style: Changes that don't affect code functionality
    - ♻️ refactor: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features
    - ⚡ perf: Performance improvements
    - 🧪 test: Adding or correcting tests
    - 🔧 chore: Changes to build process or auxiliary tools
    
  - Keep the first line (summary) under 50 characters
  - Wrap the body text at about 72 characters
  - Use a blank line to separate summary from body
  - Reference issue numbers in commit messages when applicable (e.g., "Fixes #123")
  - Use present tense ("Add feature" not "Added feature")
  - Use imperative mood ("Move cursor to..." not "Moves cursor to...")
  - Explain what and why, not how (code shows how)
  - Include relevant context that isn't obvious from the code

  Pull Requests and Code Review
  - Create pull requests for all changes to main branches
  - Use descriptive titles for pull requests
  - Include a detailed description of changes
  - Reference related issues in pull request descriptions
  - Keep pull requests focused on a single feature or fix
  - Respond to review comments promptly
  - Request reviews from appropriate team members
  - Use draft pull requests for work in progress

  Git Workflow
  - Rebase feature branches on develop before merging
  - Use squash merges for cleaner history when appropriate
  - Keep commits focused on a single logical change
  - Commit early and often during development
  - Push regularly to backup work and enable collaboration
  - Use git pull --rebase to avoid unnecessary merge commits
  - Use interactive rebase to clean up commits before merging
  - Resolve conflicts at the branch level before merging

  Git Hygiene
  - Don't commit sensitive information (passwords, API keys, etc.)
  - Don't commit large binary files (use Git LFS if necessary)
  - Don't commit generated files or dependencies
  - Keep .gitignore up to date
  - Regularly clean up stale branches
  - Don't rewrite history of shared branches
  - Use git hooks for pre-commit checks
  - Regularly backup repositories

  Collaboration Best Practices
  - Communicate about significant changes
  - Coordinate on changes to shared components
  - Avoid long-running feature branches
  - Break large features into smaller, mergeable chunks
  - Document significant architectural decisions
  - Use issues to track work and discussions
  - Keep the main branch always deployable
  - Automate testing and deployment when possible

  Git Hooks Implementation
  - Set up the following git hooks to enforce standards:
    - pre-commit: Run linters, formatters, and tests before committing
    - commit-msg: Validate commit message format
    - pre-push: Run comprehensive tests before pushing
  - Use tools like Husky for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
  - For Python projects, use pre-commit framework
  - Sample commit-msg hook to enforce Conventional Commits with emojis:
    ```bash
    #!/bin/sh
    
    commit_msg_file=$1
    commit_msg=$(cat "$commit_msg_file")
    
    # Regex pattern for conventional commits with emojis
    pattern="^(✨|🐛|📚|💎|♻️|⚡|🧪|🔧) (feat|fix|docs|style|refactor|perf|test|chore)(\([a-z0-9-]+\))?: .{1,50}$"
    
    if ! echo "$commit_msg" | head -1 | grep -E "$pattern" > /dev/null; then
      echo "ERROR: Commit message does not follow Conventional Commits format with emojis."
      echo "Format should be: <emoji> <type>[optional scope]: <description>"
      echo "Where type and emoji are one of:"
      echo "- ✨ feat: A new feature"
      echo "- 🐛 fix: A bug fix"
      echo "- 📚 docs: Documentation changes"
      echo "- 💎 style: Changes that don't affect code functionality"
      echo "- ♻️ refactor: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features"
      echo "- ⚡ perf: Performance improvements"
      echo "- 🧪 test: Adding or correcting tests"
      echo "- 🔧 chore: Changes to build process or auxiliary tools"
      exit 1
    fi
    ```
  - Install hooks automatically for all team members

  Git Tools and Extensions
  - Use git aliases for common commands
  - Consider using Git GUI tools for complex operations
  - Use git stash for temporary work
  - Use git bisect for finding bugs
  - Use git worktree for working on multiple branches
  - Consider using Git hooks for automation
  - Use git reflog to recover lost commits
  - Use git blame to understand code history

  Refer to Git documentation and GitHub/GitLab best practices for detailed information on specific Git operations and workflows.